  "id": "Compound::DB00844 - Gene::4985",
  "verbalization": "Nalbuphine is linked to Oprd1 through a series of mechanistic pathways that suggest a role in opioid receptor modulation. The evidence indicates that Nalbuphine binds to Oprk1, a heteroorganic entity, and this binding is associated with opioid receptor activity. This activity is then participated by Oprd1, suggesting a direct interaction between Nalbuphine and the opioid receptor complex. Additionally, Nalbuphine is classified as a competitive opioid antagonist, which is consistent with its ability to inhibit opioid receptor function. The inclusion of Butorphanol, a partial opioid agonist, further supports this relationship, as it binds to Oprd1 and is part of the same class of compounds. The shared lowest common ancestor between Nalbuphine and Butorphanol, along with their classification under the opioid category, reinforces the biological relevance of this interaction. The integrated evidence suggests that Nalbuphine may modulate Oprd1 through interactions with opioid receptors, potentially influencing opioid-related signaling pathways.",
